`lib/App/karr/Git.pm:478` and `lib/App/karr/BoardStore.pm:191`.

`list_task_refs` matches `m{refs/karr/tasks/(\d+)/}`, which also matches
`.../N/lock`. `load_tasks` maps `load_task_ref` over those ids with no undef
filter, so a lock ref whose `data` sibling is gone injects `undef` into the task
list and every consumer dies on it.

Reproduced without hand-crafting any refs — the lock is orphaned by a perfectly
normal failure (origin read-only, so `sync_after`'s push fails):

    karr pick --claim tester   -> dies at Cmd/Pick.pm line 170, lock NOT released
                                  refs/karr/tasks/1/lock survives
    karr delete 1 --yes        # later, task is removed
    karr list       -> Can't call method "status" on an undefined value at Cmd/List.pm line 160
    karr board      -> same, Cmd/Board.pm line 112
    karr materialize-> Can't call method "save" on an undefined value at BoardStore.pm line 239
    karr pick       -> same, Cmd/Pick.pm line 102

`karr delete 1` then reports "Task 1 not found", so **there is no way to recover
from inside karr** — it takes a manual `git update-ref -d`.

Two compounding gaps in `lib/App/karr/Lock.pm`, both verified:

- Locks have no TTL, no owner-liveness check, and there is no unlock command
  anywhere in the CLI (`App::karr::Lock` is used only by `Cmd/Pick.pm`). A stale
  lock blocks every other agent permanently — confirmed with a second agent on a
  different `user.email`, which skipped the locked task forever.
- `Cmd/Pick.pm` releases the lock *after* `sync_after`, so the release is never
  pushed to the remote even on the success path.

Fix needs three parts: filter undef out of `load_tasks` (or make
`list_task_refs` match only `/data`) so one bad ref cannot brick every command;
give locks a TTL or liveness check; release the lock before the sync that
publishes it, and expose an unlock escape hatch.
